{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 4. Predictive Analysis"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4.1. Introduction\n",
    "\n",
    "In this final notebook, we transition from historical analysis to forecasting. We will build and evaluate two different machine learning models—a Random Forest and a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) network—to predict future returns for both Bitcoin and the S&P 500. The Random Forest is an ensemble model that is powerful for tabular data, while the LSTM is a type of recurrent neural network (RNN) well-suited for time-series forecasting. Our goal is to compare their effectiveness in capturing the complex patterns of financial markets."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4.2. Import Libraries and Load Data"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import seaborn as sns\n",
    "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n",
    "from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor\n",
    "from sklearn.metrics import mean_squared_error, r2_score\n",
    "from tensorflow.keras.models import Sequential\n",
    "from tensorflow.keras.layers import LSTM, Dense, Dropout\n",
    "from sklearn.preprocessing import MinMaxScaler\n",
    "\n",
    "# Set plot style\n",
    "sns.set(style='whitegrid', palette='viridis')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "PROCESSED_DATA_FILE = '../data/processed/btc_sp500_data.csv'\n",
    "data = pd.read_csv(PROCESSED_DATA_FILE, index_col='Date', parse_dates=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Data Head:\")\n",
    "print(data.head())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4.3. Feature Engineering\n",
    "\n",
    "To prepare our data for the models, we need to create relevant features. We will use lagged returns and rolling statistics (moving averages) as predictors. The target variable will be the next day's return. This process transforms our time-series problem into a supervised learning problem."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def create_features(df, asset, lags=5, window=20):\n",
    "    df_new = pd.DataFrame()\n",
    "    df_new['price'] = df[asset]\n",
    "    df_new['returns'] = df[asset].pct_change()\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Lagged returns\n",
    "    for i in range(1, lags + 1):\n",
    "        df_new[f'lag_{i}'] = df_new['returns'].shift(i)\n",
    "        \n",
    "    # Rolling statistics\n",
    "    df_new['rolling_mean'] = df_new['returns'].rolling(window=window).mean()\n",
    "    df_new['rolling_std'] = df_new['returns'].rolling(window=window).std()\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Target variable (next day's return)\n",
    "    df_new['target'] = df_new['returns'].shift(-1)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    return df_new.dropna()\n",
    "\n",
    "btc_features = create_features(data, 'BTC-USD')\n",
    "sp500_features = create_features(data, '^GSPC')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4.4. Random Forest Model\n",
    "\n",
    "The Random Forest model is an ensemble of decision trees. It's robust and generally performs well without extensive hyperparameter tuning. We will train a separate model for each asset."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def train_rf_model(features_df):\n",
    "    X = features_df.drop(['price', 'returns', 'target'], axis=1)\n",
    "    y = features_df['target']\n",
    "    \n",
    "    X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.2, shuffle=False)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    model = RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators=100, random_state=42, n_jobs=-1)\n",
    "    model.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    predictions = model.predict(X_test)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    rmse = np.sqrt(mean_squared_error(y_test, predictions))\n",
    "    r2 = r2_score(y_test, predictions)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    return y_test, predictions, rmse, r2\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"--- Training Random Forest for Bitcoin ---")\n",
    "btc_rf_y_test, btc_rf_preds, btc_rf_rmse, btc_rf_r2 = train_rf_model(btc_features)\n",
    "print(f\"Bitcoin RF RMSE: {btc_rf_rmse:.6f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Bitcoin RF R^2 Score: {btc_rf_r2:.6f}\\\n")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"--- Training Random Forest for S&P 500 ---")\n",
    "sp500_rf_y_test, sp500_rf_preds, sp500_rf_rmse, sp500_rf_r2 = train_rf_model(sp500_features)\n",
    "print(f\"S&P 500 RF RMSE: {sp500_rf_rmse:.6f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"S&P 500 RF R^2 Score: {sp500_rf_r2:.6f}")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Visualize Random Forest Predictions"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def plot_predictions(y_test, predictions, title):\n",
    "    plt.figure(figsize=(14, 7))\n",
    "    plt.plot(y_test.index, y_test, label='Actual Returns', alpha=0.7)\n",
    "    plt.plot(y_test.index, predictions, label='Predicted Returns', alpha=0.7)\n",
    "    plt.title(title)\n",
    "    plt.xlabel('Date')\n",
    "    plt.ylabel('Returns')\n",
    "    plt.legend()\n",
    "    plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "plot_predictions(btc_rf_y_test, btc_rf_preds, 'Bitcoin Random Forest Predictions vs Actuals')\n",
    "plot_predictions(sp500_rf_y_test, sp500_rf_preds, 'S&P 500 Random Forest Predictions vs Actuals')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Interpretation\n",
    "The low R² scores (often near zero or negative) indicate that the Random Forest model, with this feature set, has very little predictive power for daily returns. The model essentially predicts the mean and fails to capture the volatility. This is a common and expected result in financial forecasting, highlighting the difficulty of predicting market movements (the Efficient Market Hypothesis in action)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4.5. LSTM Model\n",
    "\n",
    "LSTMs are designed to recognize patterns in sequences of data, making them a popular choice for time-series analysis. We need to reshape our data into a 3D format `[samples, timesteps, features]` that the LSTM layer expects."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def prepare_lstm_data(features_df, look_back=60):\n",
    "    scaler = MinMaxScaler(feature_range=(0, 1))\n",
    "    scaled_data = scaler.fit_transform(features_df.drop(['price', 'returns', 'target'], axis=1))\n",
    "    \n",
    "    X, y = [], []\n",
    "    for i in range(look_back, len(scaled_data)):\n",
    "        X.append(scaled_data[i-look_back:i])\n",
    "        y.append(features_df['target'].iloc[i])\n",
    "        \n",
    "    return np.array(X), np.array(y), features_df.index[look_back:]\n",
    "\n",
    "look_back = 60\n",
    "btc_lstm_X, btc_lstm_y, btc_lstm_index = prepare_lstm_data(btc_features, look_back)\n",
    "sp500_lstm_X, sp500_lstm_y, sp500_lstm_index = prepare_lstm_data(sp500_features, look_back)\n",
    "\n",
    "def train_lstm_model(X, y, index):\n",
    "    X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.2, shuffle=False)\n",
    "    test_index = index[len(X_train):]\n",
    "    \n",
    "    model = Sequential([\n",
    "        LSTM(50, return_sequences=True, input_shape=(X_train.shape[1], X_train.shape[2])),\n",
    "        Dropout(0.2),\n",
    "        LSTM(50, return_sequences=False),\n",
    "        Dropout(0.2),\n",
    "        Dense(25),\n",
    "        Dense(1)\n",
    "    ])\n",
    "    \n",
    "    model.compile(optimizer='adam', loss='mean_squared_error')\n",
    "    \n",
    "    model.fit(X_train, y_train, batch_size=32, epochs=20, verbose=0)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    predictions = model.predict(X_test)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    rmse = np.sqrt(mean_squared_error(y_test, predictions))\n",
    "    r2 = r2_score(y_test, predictions)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    return pd.Series(y_test, index=test_index), pd.Series(predictions.flatten(), index=test_index), rmse, r2\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"--- Training LSTM for Bitcoin ---")\n",
    "btc_lstm_y_test, btc_lstm_preds, btc_lstm_rmse, btc_lstm_r2 = train_lstm_model(btc_lstm_X, btc_lstm_y, btc_lstm_index)\n",
    "print(f\"Bitcoin LSTM RMSE: {btc_lstm_rmse:.6f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Bitcoin LSTM R^2 Score: {btc_lstm_r2:.6f}\\\n")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"--- Training LSTM for S&P 500 ---")\n",
    "sp500_lstm_y_test, sp500_lstm_preds, sp500_lstm_rmse, sp500_lstm_r2 = train_lstm_model(sp500_lstm_X, sp500_lstm_y, sp500_lstm_index)\n",
    "print(f\"S&P 500 LSTM RMSE: {sp500_lstm_rmse:.6f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"S&P 500 LSTM R^2 Score: {sp500_lstm_r2:.6f}")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Visualize LSTM Predictions"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "plot_predictions(btc_lstm_y_test, btc_lstm_preds, 'Bitcoin LSTM Predictions vs Actuals')\n",
    "plot_predictions(sp500_lstm_y_test, sp500_lstm_preds, 'S&P 500 LSTM Predictions vs Actuals')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Interpretation\n",
    "Similar to the Random Forest, the LSTM model struggles to achieve meaningful predictive accuracy, as evidenced by the low R² scores. The model's predictions appear to lag the actual returns, a common behavior where the model learns to predict something close to the previous day's value. This reinforces the concept that financial time series are notoriously difficult to predict due to their noisy and non-stationary nature."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4.6. Model Comparison and Conclusion"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "results = {\n",
    "    'Model': ['Random Forest', 'LSTM'],\n",
    "    'Bitcoin RMSE': [btc_rf_rmse, btc_lstm_rmse],\n",
    "    'Bitcoin R^2': [btc_rf_r2, btc_lstm_r2],\n",
    "    'S&P 500 RMSE': [sp500_rf_rmse, sp500_lstm_rmse],\n",
    "    'S&P 500 R^2': [sp500_rf_r2, sp500_lstm_r2]\n",
    "}\n",
    "results_df = pd.DataFrame(results)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Model Performance Comparison:\")\n",
    "print(results_df.to_string(index=False))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Final Conclusion\n",
    "\n",
    "This analysis demonstrates a core principle of financial modeling: **predicting future returns is exceptionally difficult**. Both the Random Forest and LSTM models failed to produce reliable predictions, yielding R² scores near zero. This does not mean the models are flawed, but rather that the features we engineered (lagged returns and rolling means) do not contain sufficient signal to forecast future price movements in a meaningful way.\n",
    "\n",
    "The primary takeaway is an appreciation for the Efficient Market Hypothesis, which suggests that asset prices fully reflect all available information, making it impossible to consistently outperform the market using historical data alone. While these models did not yield a profitable trading strategy, the process of building and evaluating them is a valuable exercise in understanding the limitations of predictive modeling in finance and serves as a robust demonstration of applying ML techniques to complex time-series data."
   ]
  }
 ],
